<p>NASA said Tuesday that it is moving away from plans for the Lunar Gateway space station in orbit around the moon and will instead use parts of that program for a US$20 billion base on the lunar surface. Administrator Jared Isaacman, who took over in December after President Trump appointed him, announced the change as a major redesign of the Artemis moon program. The new approach puts the focus on infrastructure for sustained operations on the moon rather than an orbital outpost. SCMP also reported that NASA is planning a nuclear-powered spacecraft for Mars. Several reports cast the shift as part of a renewed moon race involving the United States, China and Russia.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Gateway paused: One report described the move as a pause and redirection of the Lunar Gateway project toward infrastructure for long-term surface operations, including a future moon base.</li><li>Leadership change: Isaacman has led NASA since December, making this one of the first major program shifts of his tenure as administrator.</li><li>Mars ambition: SCMP reported that, alongside the lunar shift, NASA is planning a nuclear-powered spacecraft for Mars, expanding its exploration plans beyond the Artemis reset.</li><li>Geopolitical context: Deutsche Welle said the decision comes as the United States faces renewed competition in lunar exploration from Russia and China.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA leadership: Administrator Jared Isaacman presented the plan as a pivot away from an orbital station and toward infrastructure for sustained activity on the moon’s surface. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347793/nasa-abandons-orbital-lunar-station-plans-moon-base-and-nuclear-spacecraft'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Deutsche Welle: DW framed the shift as part of a renewed moon race, pointing to competitive pressure from Russia and China as a key part of the backdrop to NASA’s decision. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/nasa-plans-moon-base-instead-of-orbital-lunar-station/a-76510267'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li><li>Witness: Witness described NASA’s move as a pause in the Lunar Gateway project rather than a simple cancellation, with effort redirected toward long-term lunar surface operations. (<a href='https://witness.co.za/international/2026/03/24/nasa-to-pause-orbital-lunar-space-station-project/'>The Witness</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347793/nasa-abandons-orbital-lunar-station-plans-moon-base-and-nuclear-spacecraft'>Nasa abandons orbital station, plans moon base and nuclear spacecraft</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/nasa-plans-moon-base-instead-of-orbital-lunar-station/a-76510267'>NASA plans moon base instead of orbital lunar station</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/24/2026/nasa-aims-to-build-20b-lunar-base'>NASA aims to build $20B lunar base</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://witness.co.za/international/2026/03/24/nasa-to-pause-orbital-lunar-space-station-project/'>NASA states that it plans to pause the Lunar Gateway project and redirect efforts toward developing infrastructure for sustained operations on the Moon&#x27;s surface, including a future moonbase.</a> - witness.co.za</li></ul>